# Trivy ignore file
#
# Suppress acknowledged findings that have been reviewed and accepted.
# Format: one finding ID per line, with a comment explaining the rationale.
#
# See https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy/latest/docs/configuration/filtering/#trivyignore

# .devcontainer/Dockerfile — devcontainer-specific suppressions
DS-0002  # Not applicable: this is a devcontainer, not an application container.
         # The base image (mcr.microsoft.com/vscode/devcontainers/rust) switches
         # to the non-root 'vscode' user at runtime via its own entrypoint.
         # Adding a USER instruction here would break the devcontainer lifecycle.
DS-0026  # Not applicable: HEALTHCHECK is meaningless for a devcontainer —
         # VS Code manages the container lifecycle directly.

# projects/rust/Cargo.lock — aws-lc-sys (via aws-lc-rs → rustls)
GHSA-65p9-r9h6-22vj  # Not exploitable: timing side-channel in AES-CCM decryption
                     # (EVP_aes_128/192/256_ccm). aws-lc-sys enters via rustls, which
                     # exclusively uses AES-GCM and ChaCha20-Poly1305 cipher suites
                     # (TLS 1.3). The AES-CCM EVP CIPHER API is never invoked.
GHSA-9f94-5g5w-gf6r  # Not exploitable: logic error in CRL Distribution Point matching
                     # requires X509_V_FLAG_CRL_CHECK to be enabled. This is an
                     # OpenSSL-compatible C API flag that is structurally inaccessible
                     # through rustls's Rust API surface — no consumer of rustls can
                     # set it, even intentionally. CRL validation is performed entirely
                     # by rustls-webpki, not the AWS-LC X.509 stack.
                     # aws-lc-sys is consumed exclusively via aws-lc-rs → rustls (confirmed
                     # via `cargo tree --invert aws-lc-sys`; no other consumer exists).
                     # Advisory: https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-9f94-5g5w-gf6r
